<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the macOS bundle, applied when Tauri signs it. Referenced by
  `bundle.macOS.entitlements` in `tauri.conf.json`.

  ## Why this file exists at all

  Under the hardened runtime — which notarisation requires, and which a
  Developer ID build gets — a process cannot open the microphone unless it says
  so here, whatever `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` in `Info.plist` says. The two
  are not alternatives: the plist key is what the *user* is asked, and this
  entitlement is what the *kernel* permits. The failure mode when only the plist
  is present is the quiet one this project has already been bitten by in another
  form: everything works in `tauri dev` and in an unsigned build, then capture
  returns silence in the signed, notarised bundle.

  ## App Sandbox

  `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is here now, for the Mac App Store listing
  — HANDOVER.md has the history: the real blocker was never the sandbox
  entitlement itself but the pronunciation backend, which used to spawn
  `/usr/bin/say` and `/usr/bin/afplay`, and a sandboxed process cannot spawn
  either. `speech.rs` moved macOS onto the same in-process
  `AVSpeechSynthesizer` iOS already used, which needs no such entitlement.

  `com.apple.security.network.client` is required separately: it restricts
  *any* outgoing TCP connection under sandbox, including a raw-socket Rust
  client, not only `URLSession` traffic — `ureq` (Dropbox sync in `sync.rs`,
  the ASR/TTS model downloads in `say.rs`) is not exempt just because it does
  not go through Apple's own networking stack.

  `com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write` is what lets the
  sandboxed app use the file the learner picks in a save or open panel: the
  vocabulary and practice-log exports, the backup, the vocabulary import and
  the restore (`ask_where_to_save` and `ask_which_to_open` in `commands.rs`).
  Without it NSSavePanel/NSOpenPanel grant no access to the chosen file, and the
  write or read fails with EPERM — in exactly the build the sandbox is for. It
  covers only what the learner chose in a panel, for as long as the app runs;
  nothing here keeps a security-scoped bookmark, because nothing goes back to
  the same file later. Read-write rather than read-only, because exporting and
  backing up write.

  Nothing else needs an entitlement: the app's own files (`hanzi.db`, the
  cached models) are read and written inside the sandbox container
  automatically, and opening the Dropbox OAuth URL in the system browser needs
  none either.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
